    "updated_at": "2021-12-07T18:52:23Z",
    "body": "Unfortunately, there is no straightforward way to do this from within starship. Depending on your shell, you can write some custom logic for this.\r\n\r\nFor example, for bash, you can do the following:\r\n\r\n```\r\nORIG_PS1=\"$PS1\"\r\nrestore_prompt(){\r\n  PROMPT_COMMAND=\"\"\r\n  PS1=\"$ORIG_PS1\"\r\n}\r\neval \"$(starship init bash)\"   # Sets prompt to starship\r\nrestore_prompt                 # Sets prompt to old value\r\n```\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, it is impossible for starship to disable itself with a `starship` command because that requires settings state from the shell. We could provide an `eval` built-in, but I think it's easier just to switch *to* a different prompt (e.g. one from oh-my-zsh or the fish default) than it is to try to *undo* the effects of starship.",
